nalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37629" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}6-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -10 & -9-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (6-\\lambda )(-9-\\lambda )-(5)(-10) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable.\n\nNow, we construct the diagonal matrix $D,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37629" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4286a7677ab3c62ffb9e374117e194de0d203869cbdc668cbdd42a9e42c489bf" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37629" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37629" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37629" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37629" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37629" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37629" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$A$ is not diagonalizable"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-37629" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}10 & -12 \\\\ 8 & -10\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & ∗ \\\\ -1 & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the second column of the matrix $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -12 \\\\ 8 & -10\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -12 \\\\ 8 & -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -12 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}8 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4286a7677ab3c62ffb9e374117e194de0d203869cbdc668cbdd42a9e42c489bf" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80458" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80458" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80458" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80458" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80458" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80458" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36914" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$, \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36914" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a homogeneous system of linear equations with the matrix as above. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. On the other hand, from the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows: \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36914" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4286a7677ab3c62ffb9e374117e194de0d203869cbdc668cbdd42a9e42c489bf" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36914" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36914" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$A$ is not diagonalizable"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36914" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36914" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36914" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36914" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140055" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$, \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140055" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a homogeneous system of linear equations with the matrix as above. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{4}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{4}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140055" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4286a7677ab3c62ffb9e374117e194de0d203869cbdc668cbdd42a9e42c489bf" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140055" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140055" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$A$ is not diagonalizable"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140055" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140055" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140055" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140055" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-140055" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f4083ea4bda43a450b6af06c58e7d7137a04d6d5298ed66e9b62eb22e47eb2f" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8575" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ You're given that $\\lambda_1=4$ and $\\lambda_2=3$ are the eigenvalues of $A,$ and\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PDP^{-1} = A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/example-8575.json" "file_sha256" "43fd5c2d316ef0e745bc59410c27128c22a563652d9fb4026ce2e4fb748176aa" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8575" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}4}$ and $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}3},$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}4},$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}3}.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/example-8575.json" "file_sha256" "43fd5c2d316ef0e745bc59410c27128c22a563652d9fb4026ce2e4fb748176aa" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8572" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine if the matrix\n$[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/example-8572.json" "file_sha256" "e0821b3070bb6ff4a1372f024c8fbea60064d6b007f14c70d25c08ed4b75959d" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8572" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $A$ is triangular, the eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal. \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=3.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $A-3I\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/example-8572.json" "file_sha256" "e0821b3070bb6ff4a1372f024c8fbea60064d6b007f14c70d25c08ed4b75959d" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8573" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D=P^{-1}AP,$ find the value of $\\dfrac{a}{b}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/example-8573.json" "file_sha256" "e64fdb941f8f906c26dcfcc6097e33ec1ebd85b5b6799f30966a088dc4ad2ddc" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8573" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the diagonal matrix $[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=5$ and $\\lambda_2=-1.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-1.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_2=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the corresponding system of linear equations, we construct the augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1= -{2}x_2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBy setting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{a}{b}=\\dfrac{-2}{1} = -2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/example-8573.json" "file_sha256" "e64fdb941f8f906c26dcfcc6097e33ec1ebd85b5b6799f30966a088dc4ad2ddc" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the matrix \n$[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D=P^{-1}AP.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/example-8574.json" "file_sha256" "eb77c2dcb3324677f37f9dd4eb4ed4f5cc83576ad948685ec4a9414e7721fca6" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We apply the diagonalization algorithm. \n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=3, \\quad\\quad\\lambda_2=-2.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the corresponding system of linear equations, we construct the augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{5}{8}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). So, $x_1$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_2=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above matrix corresponds to a homogeneous system of linear equations. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=\\dfrac{8}{5}x_2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSetting  $x_2=5,$ we get the eigenvector  $[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/example-8574.json" "file_sha256" "eb77c2dcb3324677f37f9dd4eb4ed4f5cc83576ad948685ec4a9414e7721fca6" "basis" 1420 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36929" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine if the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "95d3d246a9957459037e6ddfc297438b901ac0f95d0a48387e56a1673facd815" "basis" 1919 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36929" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $A$ is triangular, the eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal. \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-4I)\\text{x}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "95d3d246a9957459037e6ddfc297438b901ac0f95d0a48387e56a1673facd815" "basis" 1919 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36929" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a635abd641512581d5b502fcd2318fc136a25a6af9112bbae6bb4d3a383af011" "basis" 1919 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36929" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "95d3d246a9957459037e6ddfc297438b901ac0f95d0a48387e56a1673facd815" "basis" 1919 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36929" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "95d3