ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "95d3d246a9957459037e6ddfc297438b901ac0f95d0a48387e56a1673facd815" "basis" 1919 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36929" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$A$ is not diagonalizable"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "95d3d246a9957459037e6ddfc297438b901ac0f95d0a48387e56a1673facd815" "basis" 1919 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -10\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & a \\\\ 2 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "95d3d246a9957459037e6ddfc297438b901ac0f95d0a48387e56a1673facd815" "basis" 1919 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ \n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Computing $A-2I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -10\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{2}{1}=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "95d3d246a9957459037e6ddfc297438b901ac0f95d0a48387e56a1673facd815" "basis" 1919 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a635abd641512581d5b502fcd2318fc136a25a6af9112bbae6bb4d3a383af011" "basis" 1919 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-