e-workers/differential/14060154/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "95d3d246a9957459037e6ddfc297438b901ac0f95d0a48387e56a1673facd815" "basis" 1919 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "95d3d246a9957459037e6ddfc297438b901ac0f95d0a48387e56a1673facd815" "basis" 1919 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "95d3d246a9957459037e6ddfc297438b901ac0f95d0a48387e56a1673facd815" "basis" 1919 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -8 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & 1 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$   find the first column of the matrix $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "95d3d246a9957459037e6ddfc297438b901ac0f95d0a48387e56a1673facd815" "basis" 1919 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-80467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$  \n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=3.$ Computing $A-3I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -8 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "95d3d246a995745