r the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -6\\end{aligned}].$ You're given that ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ are the eigenvalues of $A$, and\n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$, ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80372" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80372" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dda3ffb65010f45c2e2322883eb17b527de38d8f92e4685a9d73819ce64fc49b"} {"question" "q-80372" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80372" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -11\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80372" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80373" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the matrix $H$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nH=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ b & c\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & a\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ b & c\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}?\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80373" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nH=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn conclusion, we get  $a=-2,$ $b=7,$ and $c=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80373" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dda3ffb65010f45c2e2322883eb17b527de38d8f92e4685a9d73819ce64fc49b"} {"question" "q-80373" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80373" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$a=-2,$ $b=1,$ and $c=7$"] ["text" "$a=-2,$ $b=7,$ and $c=1$"] ["text" "$a=1,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=7$"] ["text" "$a=1,$ $b=7,$ and $c=-2$"] ["text" "$a=7,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-2$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80373" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$a=-2,$ $b=7,$ and $c=1$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-37610" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=15$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & b \\\\ 2 & c\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & b \\\\ 2 & c\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}?\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-37610" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=15,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=15.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn conclusion, we get $a=3,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-37610" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dda3ffb65010f45c2e2322883eb17b527de38d8f92e4685a9d73819ce64fc49b"} {"question" "q-37610" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-37610" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$a=-1,$ $b=3,$ and $c=-3$"] ["text" "$a=1,$ $b=-3,$ and $c=3$"] ["text" "$a=15,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-2$"] ["text" "$a=3,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=1$"] ["text" "$a=3,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-1$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-37610" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$a=3,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=1$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80389" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ You're given that ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6$ are the eigenvalues of $A$, and\n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$, ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80389" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=6.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80389" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dda3ffb65010f45c2e2322883eb17b527de38d8f92e4685a9d73819ce64fc49b"} {"question" "q-80389" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80389" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80389" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-140039" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & b \\\\ c & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & b \\\\ c & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}?\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-140039" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn conclusion, we get $a=-2,$ $b=3,$ and $c=-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-140039" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dda3ffb65010f45c2e2322883eb17b527de38d8f92e4685a9d73819ce64fc49b"} {"question" "q-140039" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-140039" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$a=-2,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=3$"] ["text" "$a=-2,$ $b=3,$ and $c=-2$"] ["text" "$a=1,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=3$"] ["text" "$a=2,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=3$"] ["text" "$a=2,$ $b=3,$ and $c=-2$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-140039" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$a=-2,$ $b=3,$ and $c=-2$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-140041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine if the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ \nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-140041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -3 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-(1)(-3) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +5+3 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable.\n\nNow, we construct the diagonal matrix $D,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-140041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dda3ffb65010f45c2e2322883eb17b527de38d8f92e4685a9d73819ce64fc49b"} {"question" "q-140041" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-140041" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "D=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["text" "$A$ is not diagonalizable"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-140041" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine if the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -12 \\\\ 4 & -7\\end{aligned}]$ \nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & -12 \\\\ 4 & -7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(-7-\\lambda )-4\\cdot (-12) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable.\n\nNow, we construct the diagonal matrix $D,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"qu