near/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-37599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dda3ffb65010f45c2e2322883eb17b527de38d8f92e4685a9d73819ce64fc49b"} {"question" "q-37599" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-37599" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["text" "$A$ is not diagonalizable"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-37599" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-36929" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine if the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-36929" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $A$ is triangular, the eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal. \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-4I)\\text{x}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-36929" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dda3ffb65010f45c2e2322883eb17b527de38d8f92e4685a9d73819ce64fc49b"} {"question" "q-36929" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-36929" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["text" "$A$ is not diagonalizable"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-36929" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$A$ is not diagonalizable"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-140053" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 15 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & 5 \\\\ b & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the value of  $\\frac{a}{b}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-140053" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$\n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 15 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 15 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 15 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{5}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{3}{1}=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-140053" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dda3ffb65010f45c2e2322883eb17b527de38d8f92e4685a9d73819ce64fc49b"} {"question" "q-140053" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-140053" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-140053" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & a \\\\ -2 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the value of $\\frac{b}{a}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Computing $A-I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.$  Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{3}{1}=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dda3ffb65010f45c2e2322883eb17b527de38d8f92e4685a9d73819ce64fc49b"} {"question" "q-80465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}10 & -12 \\\\ 8 & -10\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & ∗ \\\\ -1 & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the second column of the matrix $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -12 \\\\ 8 & -10\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -12 \\\\ 8 & -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -12 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}8 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dda3ffb65010f45c2e2322883eb17b527de38d8f92e4685a9d73819ce64fc49b"} {"question" "q-80458" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80458" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80458" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -8 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & 1 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$   find the first column of the matrix $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$  \n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=3.$ Computing $A-3I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -8 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dda3ffb65010f45c2e2322883eb17b527de38d8f92e4685a9d73819ce64fc49b"} {"question" "q-80467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -10\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & a \\\\ 2 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ \n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Computing $A-2I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -10\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{2}{1}=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dda3ffb65010f45c2e2322883eb17b527de38d8f92e4685a9d73819ce64fc49b"} {"question" "q-80464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80496" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=B,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $B=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80496" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix $B$ using its characteristic equation $det(B-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-5-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-5-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-3\\cdot (-6) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $B.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$  Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80496" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dda3ffb65010f45c2e2322883eb17b527de38d8f92e4685a9d73819ce64fc49b"} {"question" "q-80496" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80496" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$B$ is not diagonalizable"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80496" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-36914" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$, \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-36914" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a homogeneous system of linear equations with the matrix as above. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. On the other hand, from the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows: \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-36914" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dda3ffb65010f45c2e2322883eb17b527de38d8f92e4685a9d73819ce64fc49b"} {"question" "q-36914" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-36914" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$A$ is not diagonalizable"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-36914" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$, \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a homogeneous system of linear equations with the matrix as above. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dda3ffb65010f45c2e2322883eb17b527de38d8f92e4685a9d73819ce64fc49b"} {"question" "q-80493" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80493" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80493" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=B,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix $B$ using its characteristic equation $det(B-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )-2\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda +1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $B.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ={R}_{2}+(\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dda3ffb65010f45c2e2322883eb17b527de38d8f92e4685a9d73819ce64fc49b"} {"question" "q-80481" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80481" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ and\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ and\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ and\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ and\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-80481" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ and\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-140055" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$, \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-140055" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a homogeneous system of linear equations with the matrix as above. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{4}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{4}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-140055" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dda3ffb65010f45c2e2322883eb17b527de38d8f92e4685a9d73819ce64fc49b"} {"question" "q-140055" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-140055" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$A$ is not diagonalizable"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "q-140055" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/state.json" "file_sha256" "ac0a674d5f634c16b8aba0a467ff9e45f2a48666b129e9c4491b785b95a400ee"} {"question" "e-8575" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/assets/513e5adf220f2bce1441beb318dd9a731f83a292f45d51f0f8c333dbb89a9b93.png) You're given that $\\lambda_1=4$ and $\\lambda_2=3$ are the eigenvalues of $A,$ and\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/assets/6478455f2e5fcd99530dea4dae13f5df9a50208ccd53166a99249d4d5b722530.png) ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/assets/a4c0d2bb4a988598d60a45f605261c8b4d5bb30922251d821990f3464c2d56b4.png) are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PDP^{-1} = A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/example-8575.json" "file_sha256" "7e6e47a944f7652137960cf2e46b4bffeaf52918433b7c74a2f107b60726c417"} {"question" "e-8575" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}4}$ and $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}3},$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/assets/2dd66c70838ad6604661616c9bdbe3ce2e6293131084e4330b8e65dd39749f45.png)\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}4},$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}3}.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/assets/f811ea4fc7419e4afd395f1832dddc98e6b4829c6a24a34d5fd51c402767a51b.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/example-8575.json" "file_sha256" "7e6e47a944f7652137960cf2e46b4bffeaf52918433b7c74a2f107b60726c417"} {"question" "e-8572" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine if the matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/assets/dfc9ee546f72ad68cf116b8f67d165ba9b6ff6e77dce88f145dda93f13091441.png)\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/example-8572.json" "file_sha256" "9d758b027a87e97c19cfe2d9ee291d3369cfd80e198d05856de9480e5226a33c"} {"question" "e-8572" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $A$ is triangular, the eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal. \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=3.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $A-3I\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/example-8572.json" "file_sha256" "9d758b027a87e97c19cfe2d9ee291d3369cfd80e198d05856de9480e5226a33c"} {"question" "e-8573" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following matrices:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/assets/7126688b8500e60258d29ab4b1b323ac956ad41b530c5723bcc71d60b11e73a0.png)\nGiven that $D=P^{-1}AP,$ find the value of $\\dfrac{a}{b}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/example-8573.json" "file_sha256" "6bde559f492d069a8dea988b0bdd3d3e7a36c716365c90d31f666920df92e75b"} {"question" "e-8573" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the diagonal matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/assets/f3460b47901f79529216c1440403e2115ff9047cb997070893b241cd938728de.png) we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=5$ and $\\lambda_2=-1.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-1.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_2=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the corresponding system of linear equations, we construct the augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1= -{2}x_2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/assets/95d1bcb284138711bcf5afddb3f09c702e664fbdc7b256b0eaf81044f87403cf.png)\n\nBy setting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/assets/898d72a1a0cc123d1ff7ecb4c8d02f6951739a9498e36ff14ee44cb322952fb6.png)\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{a}{b}=\\dfrac{-2}{1} = -2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/example-8573.json" "file_sha256" "6bde559f492d069a8dea988b0bdd3d3e7a36c716365c90d31f666920df92e75b"} {"question" "e-8574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the matrix \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/assets/8921e351c90855b6bf113a057eaa7e3ab97fbba8f22254463d894e7c4f89a4ed.png) \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D=P^{-1}AP.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/example-8574.json" "file_sha256" "125d56ac050fb91f2fae7c96806cc4417cb23d03d5fd7cc0b5c62ecf4355099d"} {"question" "e-8574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We apply the diagonalization algorithm. \n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=3, \\quad\\quad\\lambda_2=-2.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the corresponding system of linear equations, we construct the augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{5}{8}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). So, $x_1$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_2=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/assets/ff2073ee3866cfd3b40084ed4e676cc57554d9cd02b9d6e26cb7808f127fc56c.png)\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/assets/55c840d71fa907a9b372801123fdc7f19be810f2f489a2b167b0860c84d1c2cc.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above matrix corresponds to a homogeneous system of linear equations. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=\\dfrac{8}{5}x_2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/assets/3d52ed0fc48e2e20d268b6a49b099f8e3165f25707fa36aaf93c0e8b0a2c3b16.png)\nSetting  $x_2=5,$ we get the eigenvector  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/assets/ebb90bd7e57a148040b69a9fd6fd7d4f1b253e63734617e4e57b5177d7f9cda9.png)\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/assets/4e04b534ec76c446059ca5119822b7aa299e0ea24c0e842de64a976954a733f8.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/example-8574.json" "file_sha256" "125d56ac050fb91f2fae7c96806cc4417cb23d03d5fd7cc0b5c62ecf4355099d"} {"question" "q-37599" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "94fc79f60d5772b0aecfe7ed3e4468b536d04c80c50cedba5da98331c397957f" "reviewer_session" "01a118cc-89a0-7c90-9221-d6599ae13bca" "rationale" "Derived judgment: det(A-λI)=(10-λ)(-10-λ)+96=λ²-4, giving distinct eigenvalues 2 and -2. Thus A is diagonalizable with D=diag(2,-2), exactly original option 0 and the authentic worked solution. Incorrect grading concerns submitted option 1, diag(2,4), shown under Your Answer. The stored bmatrix answer has identical entries, signs, and dimensions." "bindings" {"problem" "7341fef9b49d65ba646e85673d24748faf4cfe7094a84602921a3c9d5940504f" "worked_solution" "106aff2454713a5007d51c6af7b2c458d60aac32faaf57c44bd7be8edb83b187" "original_field" "a5efadb835d606af3f305ee4d3371e45a429959dda1cb23ce7cf5c4f99146ede" "reviewed_field" "1701c6472f634de28c92a18e59650f8b166291a3033296951e2920b92ebba71d" "raw_widget" "29beb22c69080ab051eace5187a64e1fe96ea7a706cf14bdac257e1a3fe60be9"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-37599-before.png" "sha256" "e6040c02e0b17bd0ab45178b989a7d38ebf96c31c5cca704171faf450578278d"} {"path" "history-q-37599.png" "sha256" "8ebd2ee5794e686c0d5c0b78e357087b63a26295e49c60afa1295478b47ea35c"} {"path" "q-37599-after.json" "sha256" "0716e0df4363673d72e9a138019a992a5b6a652778200b46421b37d4bc91c127"}]} {"question" "q-37599" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["text" "$A$ is not diagonalizable"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069391/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "94fc79f60d5772b0aecfe7ed3e4468b536d04c80c50cedba5da98331c397957f" "rationale" "Derived judgment: det(A-λI)=(10-λ)(-10-λ)+96=λ²-4, giving distinct eigenvalues 2 and -2. Thus A is diagonalizable with D=diag(2,-2), exactly original option 0 and the authentic worked solution. Incorrect grading concerns submitted option 1, diag(2,4), shown under Your Answer. The stored bmatrix answer has identical entries, signs, and dimensions."}] "usage" {"e-8574" [] "q-140041" [] "e-8573" [] "q-36929" [] "q-80464" [] "q-37610" [] "q-37599" [] "q-36914" [] "q-140055" [] "q-80467" [] "q-80389" [] "e-8575" [] "e-8572" [] "q-80458" [] "q-37629" []} "basis" 2368 "retractions" [[17592186125002 :question/problem "If the matrix\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}10 & -4 \\\\ 24 & -10\\end{bmatrix}$\nis diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D$."] [17592186125002 :question/answer-fields 17592186123034] [17592186124332 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ You're given that $\\lambda_1=4$ and $\\lambda_2=3$ are the eigenvalues of $A,$ and\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PDP^{-1} = A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix."] [17592186124332 :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}4}$ and $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}3},$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}4},$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}3}.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$"] [17592186124329 :question/problem "Determine if the matrix\n$[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$"] [17592186124330 :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D=P^{-1}AP,$ find the value of $\\dfrac{a}{b}.$"] [17592186124330 :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=5$ and $\\lambda_2=-1.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-1.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_2=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the corresponding system of linear equations, we construct the augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1= -{2}x_2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBy setting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{a}{b}=\\dfrac{-2}{1} = -2.$"] [17592186124331 :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D=P^{-1}AP.$"] [17592186124331 :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm. \n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=3, \\quad\\quad\\lambda_2=-2.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the corresponding system of linear equations, we construct the augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{5}{8}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). So, $x_1$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_2=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above matrix corresponds to a homogeneous system of linear equations. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=\\dfrac{8}{5}x_2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSetting  $x_2=5,$ we get the eigenvector  $[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186123035 17592186123036 17592186123037 17592186123038 17592186123039 17592186306214 17592186306215 17592186306216 17592186306217 17592186306218 17592186306221 17592186306222 17592186306223 17592186306224 17592186306225 17592186306228 17592186306229 17592186306230 17592186306231 17592186306232 17592186306281 17592186306282 17592186306283 17592186306284 17592186306285 17592186306288 17592186306289 17592186306290 17592186306291 17592186306292 17592186306302 17592186306303 17592186306304 17592186306305 17592186306306 17592186306337 17592186306338 17592186306339 17592186306340 17592186306341 17592186338919 17592186338920 17592186338921 17592186338922 17592186338923 17592186338932 17592186338933 17592186338934 17592186338935 17592186338936 17592186338939 17592186338940 17592186338941 17592186338942 17592186338943] "immutable_fields" [[17592186123034 :answer-field/correct] [17592186123034 :answer-field/id] [17592186123034 :answer-field/key] [17592186123034 :answer-field/type] [17592186306213 :answer-field/correct] [17592186306213 :answer-field/id] [17592186306213 :answer-field/key] [17592186306213 :answer-field/type] [17592186306220 :answer-field/correct] [17592186306220 :answer-field/id] [17592186306220 :answer-field/key] [17592186306220 :answer-field/type] [17592186306227 :answer-field/correct] [17592186306227 :answer-field/id] [17592186306227 :answer-field/key] [17592186306227 :answer-field/type] [17592186306280 :answer-field/correct] [17592186306280 :answer-field/id] [17592186306280 :answer-field/key] [17592186306280 :answer-field/type] [17592186306287 :answer-field/correct] [17592